{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And the people spoke against Elohim and against Moses, etc.</b> It appears that they spoke not against the manna alone but also against the water, for they said, ״אֵין לֶחֶם וְאֵין מַיִם״ — for the bread and the water were both spiritual and light, and all light things are good for those at rest but not for wayfarers, for heavy food is good for digestion for those who labor. Therefore when the people's spirit grew short from the length of the journey, they said according to the burden of the road, ״אֵין לֶחֶם וְאֵין מַיִם״, for both appear as though they are nothing and were as if they did not exist, ״וְנַפְשֵׁנוּ קָצָה בַּלֶּחֶם הַקְּלֹקֵל״ — קַל קַל, both of them light, as stated. And corresponding to their speaking against the food, serpents were sent against them, for it is said of it (Isaiah 65:25), ״וְנָחָשׁ עָפָר לַחְמוֹ״. And corresponding to their speaking against the water, the fiery serpents were sent against them, for there is no water to extinguish the fire." "And in Toldot Yitzchak he wrote: Because they spoke against Elohim and against Moses, the Holy One, blessed be He, said: My portion is forgiven, but — 'Make yourself a serpent' corresponding to what they did to you. And Moses said the reverse: My portion is forgiven, but the portion on high — who can grant? Therefore, ״וַיַּעַשׂ נְחַשׁ נְחֹשֶׁת״. And the root of this matter is also found in the Yalkut, parashat Shemot. Explanation according to his explanation: for the evil tongue burns and harms a person, for ״חִצֵּי גִבּוֹר שְׁנוּנִים עִם גַּחֲלֵי רְתָמִים״ (Psalms 120:4); therefore the fiery serpent came corresponding to what they spoke against Moses. But toward above, ״מַה יִּתֵן לְךָ וּמַה יּוֹסִיף לָשׁוֹן רְמִיָּה״ (Psalms 120:3); therefore the serpents were sent against them, because they took up the craft of the primordial serpent, which told evil tongue about its Creator — for tongue falls upon tongue: the serpent that sinned with the tongue falls upon masters of the tongue. ״וְהִבִּיט אֶל נְחַשׁ הַנְּחֹשֶׁת וָחָי״ — that he may see what happened to the primordial serpent, and then he will not return to his folly."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The phrase קַל קַל plays on the root ק-ל (light) in קְלֹקֵל and קַלִּים; both renderings preserved to keep the wordplay visible.", 1 "'Toldot Yitzchak' refers to R. Yitzchak Karo's commentary; the play on לשון (tongue/serpent's speech) is preserved throughout without smoothing."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:24.334592224Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}